1. @PatchMapping – For Partial Updates
📌 Use Case:
You want to update only selected fields (e.g., just the status field of a task) instead of the full PUT update.

✨ Example: Update only status using PATCH
🔁 URL:
http
Copy
Edit
PATCH /api/tasks/{id}/status
✅ Controller:
java
Copy
Edit
@PatchMapping("/tasks/{id}/status")
public ResponseEntity<String> updateTaskStatus(@PathVariable Long id,
                                               @RequestParam Status status) {
    taskService.updateStatus(id, status);
    return ResponseEntity.ok("Task status updated to " + status);
}
✅ Service Method:
java
Copy
Edit
public void updateStatus(Long id, Status status) {
    TaskEntity task = repo.findById(id)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new TaskNotFoundException(id));

    task.setStatus(status); // Partial update
    repo.save(task);
}
✅ Output:
PATCH Request:

bash
Copy
Edit
PATCH /api/tasks/5/status?status=COMPLETED
✔️ Only the status field changes!
❌ No need to send full DTO with all fields.

Note: @PatchMapping is REST-style way of saying “partial update” — unlike @PutMapping, which expects full resource replacement.

✅ 2. Where to use @Transactional?
🎯 What it does:
Opens a DB transaction

Commits only if method finishes successfully

Rolls back automatically if an exception is thrown

📍 Best Practice – Apply on Service layer methods
java
Copy
Edit
@Service
public class TaskServiceImpl implements TaskService {

    @Transactional
    public void updateStatus(Long id, Status status) {
        TaskEntity task = repo.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new TaskNotFoundException(id));

        task.setStatus(status);
        // ✅ No explicit save() needed if persistence context is active
    }
}
💡 Use @Transactional when:
Use-case	Use it?
🔄 Multiple DB operations	✅ Yes
✂️ Partial update via PATCH	✅ Yes
💣 Risk of exception mid-update	✅ Yes
💤 Read-only GET methods	❌ No

🧠 Summary Cheat Sheet
Feature	Best Practice
@PatchMapping	Use for partial field updates, e.g., just status
@Transactional	Place on service-layer methods doing DB changes

