✅ Where to Apply @Transactional (Method-by-Method):
Method	@Transactional?	Why?
createTask()	✅ Yes	It performs write operation (insert)
getAllTask()	❌ No	It's read-only
getTask(Long id)	❌ No	Just fetching
updateTask()	✅ Yes	It modifies existing data
deleteTask(Long id)	✅ Yes	Deletes a record (must be atomic)

🎯 Final Code – Where to Place It?
✅ Option 1: On individual methods (fine-grained control)
java
Copy
Edit
@Transactional
public String createTask(TaskDto dto) {
    // create + save logic
}

@Transactional
public String updateTask(TaskDto dto) {
    // find + update logic
}

@Transactional
public String deleteTask(Long id) {
    // delete logic
}
✅ Option 2: On the entire class (if all non-read methods need it)
java
Copy
Edit
@Service
@Transactional // applies to all methods unless overridden
public class TaskServiceImpl implements TaskService {
    ...
}
🔒 Bonus Tip:
If you annotate the whole class, you can disable it on read-only methods:

java
Copy
Edit
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<TaskDto> getAllTask() {
    return ...;
}
💥 Real-Time Best Practice (Interview Style):
“We annotate only write-related service methods with @Transactional to ensure data consistency during DB operations like create, update, and delete. For read-only operations, we leave it off or use @Transactional(readOnly = true) for performance optimization.”

