🔥 Nathan baavaaa!
This is a classic interview vs real-time combo question — and since you're working on REST APIs now, you must master this difference! 🧠⚔️

Let’s break it down simply, visually, and like a backend king 👑

💥 1. Path Variable (@PathVariable)
🔁 Used in the URL path
Represents a part of the URI itself

✅ Example:
java
Copy
Edit
@GetMapping("/tasks/{id}")
public ResponseEntity<TaskDto> getTask(@PathVariable Long id)
📥 URL Call:

bash
Copy
Edit
GET /tasks/10
🎯 Output:

java
Copy
Edit
id = 10
📌 Use-case:
Entity IDs

Anything uniquely identifying the resource

Part of the RESTful route

🌱 2. Request Param (@RequestParam)
🔁 Used in the query string after ?
✅ Example:
java
Copy
Edit
@GetMapping("/tasks/search")
public ResponseEntity<List<TaskDto>> findTasks(@RequestParam String status)
📥 URL Call:

sql
Copy
Edit
GET /tasks/search?status=COMPLETED
🎯 Output:

java
Copy
Edit
status = "COMPLETED"
📌 Use-case:
Filters, search, pagination

Optional or configurable values

Not part of the resource identity

🔍 Visual Difference:
Type	Looks Like	Used For
@PathVariable	/tasks/5	Resource Identification
@RequestParam	/tasks?status=COMPLETED	Filtering / Optional Params

🚀 Real-Time Example: Using Both
java
Copy
Edit
@GetMapping("/tasks/{id}/update-status")
public ResponseEntity<String> updateStatus(@PathVariable Long id,
                                           @RequestParam Status status) {
    taskService.updateStatus(id, status);
    return ResponseEntity.ok("Updated!");
}
📥 Call:

bash
Copy
Edit
PATCH /tasks/42/update-status?status=COMPLETED
🧠 Means:
👉 “Update task whose ID is 42 to COMPLETED”

🧠 One-Liner Summary (For Interviews):
PathVariable is used when the value is essential to locate the resource (like id).
RequestParam is used for optional inputs like filters, sorting, and search criteria.
