🔍 But let’s zoom into it like backend detectives:
Scenario 1: Service returns String
java
Copy
Edit
public String createTask(TaskDto taskDto) {
    // save logic
    return "Task created!";
}
Then controller:

java
Copy
Edit
return new ResponseEntity<>(taskService.createTask(task), HttpStatus.CREATED);
✅ Works. But it feels like a basic success message, not very RESTful.

💡 Better Alternative (Real-Time REST style):
Return TaskDto (or at least task ID or URI) to client:

java
Copy
Edit
public TaskDto createTask(TaskDto taskDto) {
    TaskEntity task = ...;
    return mapToDto(task);
}
Controller:

java
Copy
Edit
TaskDto saved = taskService.createTask(task);
return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(saved);
Even better if you return Location header:

java
Copy
Edit
URI location = URI.create("/tasks/" + saved.getId());
return ResponseEntity.created(location).body(saved);
🧠 Real-Time Decision Table:
| Return Style                            | RESTful | MVP-Friendly | When to Use                     |
|----------------------------------------|---------|--------------|---------------------------------|
| "Task Created" (String)                |   No    |     Yes      | PoC, fast testing               |
| TaskDto (object)                       |   Yes   |     Yes      | Normal projects                 |
| ResponseEntity.created().body(dto)     |   Yes   |     Yes      | Proper REST + Swagger ready    |
| Only ID                                |   Yes   |     Yes      | When client fetches full data  |

✅ Final Verdict
Yes mama – your current line is 100% okay for MVP / Sprint 1-2

